To re-run failed test cases in Selenium (Java) using TestNG, there are multiple commonly used approaches.
1. Manual Run
2. Using Retry Logic

Manual Run:
Using TestNG testng-failed.xml (Easiest Way)
After a TestNG run: TestNG automatically generates a file:
test-output/testng-failed.xml

👉 Steps:
Run your test suite
Navigate to: test-output → testng-failed.xml
Right-click and run this file as TestNG suite

Using IRetryAnalyzer (Retry Automatically)
1. Create Retry Analyzer class
2. Add Retry in Test Method
3. Apply Retry to All Tests Using Listener

TestNG documentation:
https://testng.org/#_rerunning_failed_tests

IRetryAnalyzer:
TestNG interface used to control whether a failed test should be re-executed or not.
boolean retry(ITestResult result): This method is called automatically by TestNG whenever a test fails
                                   You return:
                                   true → 🔁 rerun the test
                                   false → ❌ do not rerun
ITestResult:  is an interface that contains information about the test result
It gives details like:
Test name
Status (PASS / FAIL / SKIP)
Exception
Execution time
Class name

IAnnotationTransformer: Dynamically attach retry logic to ALL test methods automatically
Reduces : @Test(retryAnalyzer = MyRetry.class) annotation to all the test methods
